 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "What is Form 210 in Spain?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Form 210, also called Modelo 210, is used to declare Spanish-source income obtained by non-residents without a permanent establishment. For a property owner, it can cover rent, imputed income for periods when an urban property is not let, or the result of a sale. These are different types of income with different filing rules; owning a property does not by itself make you tax resident in Spain."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What if I have not filed for several years?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "First we check which returns are outstanding and the applicable time limits. For overdue returns with tax to pay filed without a prior request from the Tax Agency, article 27 of the General Tax Act provides a surcharge of 1% plus 1% for each full month of delay. After twelve months, the surcharge is 15%, with late-payment interest from the day after those twelve months end until filing. This surcharge regime excludes a penalty for the late filing it covers; nil returns and cases where the Tax Agency has already acted need a separate assessment."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Do I need a tax representative in Spain?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Not always. If you are not resident in the EU or in an EEA State with mutual assistance rules, you must appoint one in certain cases, for example when you operate through a permanent establishment, or when the Tax Agency requires it because of the amount of your income or because you own property in Spain. For residents of other European Economic Area countries, that obligation does not apply where there is mutual assistance on tax information and collection (Non-Resident Income Tax Act, article 10). Even when it is optional, it is useful: someone in Spain receives the notices and can act within the deadlines they open."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can these filings be done without coming to Spain?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. We handle them remotely under your power of attorney; your digital certificate stays with you."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can you file my Modelo 210 if I live in the UK and do not have a Spanish digital certificate?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. Grupo Avanza Consultores prepares and files Modelo 210 for non-resident owners of property in Spain. When a representative authorised to file on someone else’s behalf submits the return, it is the representative who needs the electronic certificate, not you (Order EHA/3316/2010, article 12.1.c). We first check the property’s use, ownership and tax year, then explain the authorisation you need to give us. Tell us where you are tax resident, which years need attention and whether the property was rented out. We agree the scope and fee before starting and reply in writing in English."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Is Form 210 the same as Model 210?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. Form 210, Model 210 and Modelo 210 are the same return: the self-assessment approved by Order EHA/3316/2010 for non-residents who obtain income in Spain without a permanent establishment (article 1)."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can I file Modelo 210 online from abroad?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. It is filed online at the Tax Agency’s electronic office with a recognised electronic certificate or, if you are an individual, with Cl@ve. Whoever files needs a Spanish tax number (NIF) and must be registered in the tax census beforehand. A representative authorised to file for you can submit it with their own certificate (Order EHA/3316/2010, article 12)."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Do non-residents pay property tax on a Spanish home they don’t rent out?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes, and there are two different taxes. The local council charges the annual property tax (IBI) to the holder of the property or of the other rights that trigger it, usually the owner (Local Finance Act, article 61). Separately, if you are an individual and the home is an urban property neither let nor used for a business, non-resident income tax applies to an imputed income, declared on Modelo 210 (Non-Resident Income Tax Act, articles 13.1.h and 24.5). For 2026, that income is 2% of the cadastral value, or 1.1% where the property is in a municipality whose cadastral values were revised, modified or set under a general collective valuation procedure that came into force on or after 1 January 2012, in proportion to the days owned (different rules apply if there is no cadastral value or it has not been notified, and no income is imputed to buildings under construction or not usable for town-planning reasons) (Personal Income Tax Act, article 85 and additional provision fifty-five, as worded by Royal Decree-law 29/2026, validated by the Congress of Deputies on 12 November 2026). The rate is 19% for residents of the EU, or of an EEA country with an effective exchange of tax information, and 24% for everyone else (Non-Resident Income Tax Act, article 25.1.a). The percentages for 2027 income have been amended, so we confirm the rule for each year."}}]}</script>
