     <p>Rent for April to June 2026 filed separately was due by 20 July; if it was not filed, it is now late. Rent with tax to pay for October to December 2026 filed separately, all 2026 rent with tax to pay filed grouped, and imputed income for 2026 follow the new deadlines: 1 to 20 April 2027 for rent, and 1 April to 31 December 2027 for imputed income.</p>
+    <h2>What you declare depending on how the property is used</h2>
+    <p>Modelo 210 covers different kinds of income, and what you file depends on what you do with the property (Spanish Tax Agency: <a href="https://sede.agenciatributaria.gob.es/Sede/no-residentes/irnr-sin-establecimiento-permanente/cuestiones-especificas-sobre-tributacion-inmuebles.html">types of income from property</a>, in Spanish, consulted on 18 November 2026):</p>
+    <ul>
+      <li><strong>Own use or empty:</strong> imputed income once a year, based on the cadastral value; it is explained in the next section.</li>
+      <li><strong>Letting:</strong> the rental income, with the deadlines above. More in <a href="renting-out-property-spain-non-resident.html">renting out a property in Spain as a non-resident</a>.</li>
+      <li><strong>Let for part of the year only:</strong> you declare the rental income for the period it was let and imputed income for the rest. The Tax Agency states that imputed income is reduced in proportion to the number of days when the property has been let for part of the year (<a href="https://sede.agenciatributaria.gob.es/Sede/en_gb/no-residentes/irnr-sin-establecimiento-permanente/cuestiones-especificas-sobre-tributacion-inmuebles/renta-imputada-inmueble-urbano-uso-propio.html">imputed income on urban property for own use</a>).</li>
+      <li><strong>Sale:</strong> the buyer withholds 3% of the price and pays it in with Modelo 211 (article 25.2 of the Non-Resident Income Tax Law); you declare the gain on Modelo 210, and the town hall may also charge the municipal plusvalía (article 59.2 of the Local Finances Law). Guides: <a href="selling-property-spain-non-resident.html">selling property in Spain as a non-resident</a>, <a href="buying-property-from-non-resident-spain.html">buying from a non-resident and Modelo 211</a> and <a href="plusvalia-tax-spain-non-resident.html">plusvalía municipal tax for non-residents</a>.</li>
+    </ul>
     <h2>An empty property must also be declared</h2>
     <p>If a non-resident individual owns an urban property in Spain that is neither let nor used for a business, the law, with limited exceptions, imputes an income to it and it must be declared, even if the property is closed all year (Non-Resident Income Tax Act, Royal Legislative Decree 5/2004, articles 13.1.h and 24.5). It is not a fine; it is how the tax works. And each year left undeclared remains pending until it is regularised or becomes time-barred.</p>
     <p>For a Spanish holiday home you use yourself, look for <i lang="es">valor catastral</i> on your Spanish property tax (IBI) bill. The <i lang="es">referencia catastral</i> on that bill is the code that identifies the property. Belgium assigns its own cadastral income (<i lang="nl">kadastraal inkomen</i>) to foreign property. A Dutch tax return may use the market value of a second home abroad. For Modelo 210, use the <a href="https://sede.agenciatributaria.gob.es/Sede/en_gb/no-residentes/irnr-sin-establecimiento-permanente/cuestiones-especificas-sobre-tributacion-inmuebles/renta-imputada-inmueble-urbano-uso-propio.html">Spanish Tax Agency’s valuation rules</a>. If you have never been notified of a Spanish cadastral value, mention this when you <a href="#contact">request a quote</a>. See also our <a href="modelo-210-woning-spanje-documenten.html" hreflang="nl">document checklist in Dutch</a>.</p>
